WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.031 --> 00:00:01.913
Every week, there's a new tool with a

2
00:00:01.973 --> 00:00:03.755
catchy name and a bold claim.

3
00:00:04.376 --> 00:00:05.698
It can feel overwhelming.

4
00:00:06.278 --> 00:00:08.081
But here's the thing: they’re not all

5
00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:09.202
doing something different.

6
00:00:09.823 --> 00:00:11.525
Most of them fit into one of five

7
00:00:11.605 --> 00:00:12.005
layers.

8
00:00:12.666 --> 00:00:14.748
Once you learn these layers, every new

9
00:00:14.788 --> 00:00:16.871
name you hear will just be another tool

10
00:00:16.971 --> 00:00:18.253
in a familiar category.

11
00:00:18.873 --> 00:00:21.196
It’s like saying, 'Oh, that’s another

12
00:00:21.216 --> 00:00:21.837
one of those.'

13
00:00:22.638 --> 00:00:24.520
It makes it easier to keep up.

14
00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:27.053
The first layer is where you describe your

15
00:00:27.113 --> 00:00:27.533
ideas.

16
00:00:28.274 --> 00:00:30.097
These are plain chat assistants.

17
00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:32.219
You type what you want in everyday

18
00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:32.720
language.

19
00:00:33.381 --> 00:00:35.584
They’re great for brainstorming, naming,

20
00:00:35.984 --> 00:00:37.767
planning, and writing the brief you’ll

21
00:00:37.827 --> 00:00:39.229
use everywhere else.

22
00:00:39.629 --> 00:00:41.711
Just remember, they don’t build or host

23
00:00:41.791 --> 00:00:42.172
anything.

24
00:00:42.613 --> 00:00:44.475
They help you sharpen your idea.

25
00:00:45.113 --> 00:00:47.395
Now, layer two is where it gets built.

26
00:00:47.856 --> 00:00:49.457
There are two flavors here, and

27
00:00:49.517 --> 00:00:51.139
understanding the difference is key.

28
00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.661
App builders run in your browser.

29
00:00:54.182 --> 00:00:56.244
You describe an app, it appears, and you

30
00:00:56.284 --> 00:00:57.204
keep talking to it.

31
00:00:57.765 --> 00:00:59.226
Nothing needs to be installed.

32
00:00:59.747 --> 00:01:01.008
This is where you should start.

33
00:01:01.508 --> 00:01:03.070
Then there are AI editors.

34
00:01:03.671 --> 00:01:05.512
They run on your computer and work with

35
00:01:05.592 --> 00:01:06.293
real files.

36
00:01:06.854 --> 00:01:08.795
They offer more power but come with more

37
00:01:08.855 --> 00:01:09.456
complexity.

38
00:01:10.057 --> 00:01:13.039
We’ll dive into that in lesson five.

39
00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:15.404
Layer three is where it looks right.

40
00:01:16.085 --> 00:01:18.510
Design tools help decide how your app will

41
00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:19.612
look before it’s built.

42
00:01:20.254 --> 00:01:22.077
You can skip this layer and let the

43
00:01:22.117 --> 00:01:24.442
builder pick the design, but it’ll look

44
00:01:24.502 --> 00:01:26.647
generic — competent but forgettable.

45
00:01:27.208 --> 00:01:29.292
Spending just an hour here can make a

46
00:01:29.392 --> 00:01:31.817
big difference, especially when your app

47
00:01:31.937 --> 00:01:33.561
is in front of people whose opinions

48
00:01:33.621 --> 00:01:34.302
matter.

49
00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:37.363
Next is layer four, where the data lives.

50
00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:40.307
A database is basically a set of tables.

51
00:01:40.987 --> 00:01:42.830
Think of a table as a spreadsheet with

52
00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:43.270
rules.

53
00:01:44.011 --> 00:01:45.533
This is where everything your app

54
00:01:45.573 --> 00:01:47.796
remembers between visits is stored —

55
00:01:47.796 --> 00:01:50.599
accounts, orders, posts, and settings.

56
00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.843
Beginners often skip this layer and end up

57
00:01:53.903 --> 00:01:55.125
having to rebuild later.

58
00:01:55.886 --> 00:01:57.988
Lesson seven is all about getting this

59
00:01:58.068 --> 00:01:58.669
right.

60
00:01:59.392 --> 00:02:01.756
Finally, layer five is where it runs.

61
00:02:02.477 --> 00:02:04.661
Hosting is a computer somewhere else that

62
00:02:04.741 --> 00:02:07.085
stays on so people can access your app.

63
00:02:07.686 --> 00:02:09.850
A domain is the readable address that

64
00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:10.551
points to it.

65
00:02:11.192 --> 00:02:13.236
Until you set this up, what you’ve built

66
00:02:13.356 --> 00:02:15.139
is only available to you.

67
00:02:15.777 --> 00:02:17.180
Let’s read the map backwards.

68
00:02:17.801 --> 00:02:20.087
The layers also show the order things can

69
00:02:20.127 --> 00:02:21.951
go wrong, from bottom to top.

70
00:02:22.552 --> 00:02:24.156
If hosting breaks, it’s loud.

71
00:02:24.657 --> 00:02:26.120
If data breaks, it’s worse.

72
00:02:26.661 --> 00:02:28.144
Design issues are quieter.

73
00:02:28.666 --> 00:02:31.171
When something goes wrong, ask which layer

74
00:02:31.231 --> 00:02:32.995
it’s in before figuring out what to do

75
00:02:33.055 --> 00:02:34.237
next.

76
00:02:34.237 --> 00:02:35.961
So, where do you start?

77
00:02:36.562 --> 00:02:38.266
If you have just an idea, go to

78
00:02:38.326 --> 00:02:40.110
layer two, the browser flavor.

79
00:02:40.831 --> 00:02:43.076
If you already have a design, use layer

80
00:02:43.096 --> 00:02:44.580
two, the editor flavor.

81
00:02:45.221 --> 00:02:46.905
If you have something working but nobody

82
00:02:46.945 --> 00:02:49.009
can see it, jump to layer five.

83
00:02:49.611 --> 00:02:51.715
Everything else is just optimization.

84
00:02:52.898 --> 00:02:53.519
What’s next?

85
00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:55.544
In the next lesson, we’ll take one

86
00:02:55.624 --> 00:02:57.488
sentence and turn it into a working

87
00:02:57.588 --> 00:02:58.210
application.

88
00:02:58.911 --> 00:03:00.695
That’s the promise of this course, and

89
00:03:00.715 --> 00:03:01.918
we’re going to demonstrate it.

90
00:03:02.619 --> 00:03:03.481
Get ready!

